.TH "libstring" "7" "July 2026" "BetterScripts libstring v1.0.0" "Text processing helpers for POSIX.1 shells."
.hy
.SS LIBSTRING
.SS SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[I]Full synopsis, description, arguments, examples and other
information is\f[R] \f[I]documented with each individual command.\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]string_length [<OUTPUT>] <STRING>\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]string_quote [<OUTPUT>] <STRING>\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]string_join [<OUTPUT>] <STRING>...\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]string_toupper [<OUTPUT>] <STRING>\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]string_tolower [<OUTPUT>] <STRING>\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]string_trim [<OUTPUT>] <STRING> <LENGTH>\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]string_index [<OPTION>] [--] [<OUTPUT>] <STRING> <STRING>\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]string_match [<OPTION>] [--] <STRING> <STRING>\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]string_substr <OUTPUT> <STRING> <OFFSET> [<LENGTH>]\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]string_truncate [<OUTPUT>] <STRING> <LENGTH>\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]string_sub [<OPTION>] [--] [<OUTPUT>] <STRING> <LENGTH>\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]string_gsub [<OPTION>] [--] [<OUTPUT>] <STRING> <LENGTH>\f[R]
.SS DESCRIPTION
.PP
Provides commands for \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] compliant shell to assist
processing text safely and robustly.
.SS EXIT STATUS
.IP \[bu] 2
For all commands the exit status will be \f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]<zero>\f[R])
if, and only if, the command was completed successfully.
.IP \[bu] 2
For any command which is intended to perform a test, an exit status of
\f[V]1\f[R] (\f[V]<one>\f[R]) indicates \[lq]false\[rq], while
\f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]<zero>\f[R]) indicates \[lq]true\[rq].
.IP \[bu] 2
An exit status that is \f[I]NOT\f[R] \f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]<zero>\f[R]) from
an external command will be propagated to the caller where relevant (and
possible).
.IP \[bu] 2
Exit status\[cq] not covered by any of the above use values as described
in FreeBSD
\f[V]SYSEXITS(3)\f[R] (https://www.freebsd.org/cgi/man.cgi?sysexits(3))
- including the \f[V]EX_USAGE\f[R] which is used for all usage errors.
.IP \[bu] 2
Exit status is configuration agnostic.
.SS ENVIRONMENT
.PP
A number of environment variables affect the library, these are split
into variables that instruct the library to work-around specific
platform issues, and variables that convey user preferences.
Variables that enable platform specific work-arounds will be
automatically set if needed, but can also be set manually to force
specific configurations.
.PP
In additional to these, there are a number of variables that are set by
the library to convey information outside of command invocation.
.PP
If unset, some variables will take an initial value from a common
\f[V]shtoolkit\f[R] variable applicable to all libraries, these allow
the same configuration to be used across libraries more easily.
.PP
After the library has been sourced, external commands must not set
library environment variables that are classified as \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R].
Variables may use the \f[V]readonly\f[R] command to enforce this.
.PP
\f[B]\f[BI]If not otherwise specified, an \f[VBI]<unset>\f[BI] variable
is equivalent to the\f[B]\f[R] \f[B]\f[BI]default value.\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[I]For more details see the \f[VI]shtoolkit\f[I] general
documentation.\f[R]
.SS PLATFORM CONFIGURATION
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_NO_Z_SHELL_SETOPT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Suite: \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_Z_SHELL_SETOPT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
[Disable]/Enable using \f[V]setopt\f[R] in \f[I]Z Shell\f[R] to ensure
\f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] like behavior.
.IP \[bu] 2
Automatically enabled if \f[I]Z Shell\f[R] is detected.
.IP \[bu] 2
Any use of \f[V]setopt\f[R] is scoped as tightly as possible and SHOULD
not affect other commands.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_NO_DEV_NULL\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Suite: \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_DEV_NULL\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
[Disable]/Enable using alternatives to \f[V]/dev/null\f[R] as a
redirection source/target (e.g.\ for output suppression).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: Use \f[V]/dev/null\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: Use an alternative to \f[V]/dev/null\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Using \f[V]/dev/null\f[R] as a redirection target is a common idiom, but
not always possible (e.g.
restricted shells generally forbid this), the alternative is to capture
output (and ignore it) but this is much slower as it involves a
subshell.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_SHELL_SUPPORTS_MBC\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Suite: \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_SHELL_SUPPORTS_MBC\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
Disable/[Enable] support for multi-byte character processing within the
shell itself.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: use fallback code for operations affected.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: use internal shell operations.
.IP \[bu] 2
Default is to run tests for the current shell when a library is sourced
to determine if such support is present.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]WARNING:\f[R] Automatic tests assume the locale has not been set
since the current shell was invoked - if this is not the case
configuration will be incorrect.
.IP \[bu] 2
See \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_SHELL_SUPPORTS_MBC\f[R] for details.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_SHELL_SUPPORTS_PORTABLE_GLOB\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Suite: \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_SHELL_SUPPORTS_PORTABLE_GLOB\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
Disable/[Enable] glob/wildcard pattern matching even if the pattern
contains known problematic characters.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: use fallback code for patterns that contain problem
characters.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: use shell pattern matching.
.IP \[bu] 2
Default is to run tests for the current shell when a library is sourced
to determine if the current shell supports these as expected or not.
.IP \[bu] 2
See \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_SHELL_SUPPORTS_PORTABLE_GLOB\f[R] for
details.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_NO_PARAM_EXPANSION_CHAR_CLASS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Suite: \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_PARAM_EXPANSION_CHAR_CLASS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
Disable/[Enable] using \f[V][:...:]\f[R] character classes in parameter
expansions.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: use fallback code.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: use character classes.
.IP \[bu] 2
Default is to use character classes if possible.
.IP \[bu] 2
Modern shell implementations generally support using character classes
in parameter expansions, which results in much better performance than
the alternatives.
.IP \[bu] 2
See \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_PARAM_EXPANSION_CHAR_CLASS\f[R] for
details.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_NO_CASE_CHAR_CLASS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Suite: \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_CASE_CHAR_CLASS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
Disable/[Enable] using \f[V][:...:]\f[R] character classes in
\f[V]case\f[R] matches.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: use fallback code.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: use character classes.
.IP \[bu] 2
Default is to use character classes if possible.
.IP \[bu] 2
Modern shell implementations generally support using character classes
in \f[V]case\f[R] matches, which results in much better performance than
the alternatives.
.IP \[bu] 2
See \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_CASE_CHAR_CLASS\f[R] for details.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_TR_SUPPORTS_MBC\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Suite: \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_TR_SUPPORTS_MBC\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic> (delayed)
.IP \[bu] 2
Disable/[Enable] the use of \f[V]tr\f[R] outside the \f[I]POSIX\f[R]
locale.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: do not use \f[V]tr\f[R] outside the \f[I]POSIX\f[R]
locale.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: use \f[V]tr\f[R] for all locales.
.IP \[bu] 2
Some implementations of \f[V]tr\f[R] support \f[I]ONLY\f[R] single- byte
characters.
Since it is difficult to determine if a string contains only supported
characters, it is assumed that if the locale is not the \f[I]POSIX\f[R]
locale that \f[I]ALL\f[R] strings contain multi-byte characters and so
can not be correctly used with these versions of \f[V]tr\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
See \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_TR_SUPPORTS_MBC\f[R] for details.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_ALLOW_EXPR\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[I]OFF\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
[Disable]/Enable] the use of \f[V]expr\f[R] in some cases.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: do not use \f[V]expr\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: use \f[V]expr\f[R] by preference, fallback if it fails.
.IP \[bu] 2
For \f[I]Basic Regular Expressions\f[R] \f[V]expr\f[R] is often the best
performing utility available, however, it is also subject to significant
portability issues that make it hard to use in the general case,
therefore code that uses \f[V]expr\f[R] also needs fallback code to
handle cases where it fails.
.IP \[bu] 2
When this variable is \f[I]OFF\f[R], \f[V]expr\f[R] is not used and
fallback code is used in all cases - this is safer, but loses some
performance.
.IP \[bu] 2
When this variable is \f[I]ON\f[R], fallback code is only used if
\f[V]expr\f[R] fails - this is faster in general, but slower if
\f[V]expr\f[R] always fails.
.IP \[bu] 2
Note that currently the use of \f[V]expr\f[R] is limited and will often
be avoided entirely.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_NO_AWK_ARGV\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Suite: \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_AWK_ARGV\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]VARIABLE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[V]0\f[R] (Use \f[V]awk\f[R] \f[V]ARGV\f[R])
.IP \[bu] 2
Disable/[Enable] using \f[V]ARGV\f[R] within \f[V]awk\f[R] - enabling
gives significantly better performance, but is subject to some
limitations.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: Use \f[V]ARGV\f[R] within \f[V]awk\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: Avoid \f[V]ARGV\f[R] within \f[V]awk\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
When \f[I]ON\f[R] \f[V]ARGV\f[R] will be used whenever appropriate, if
this fails (likely due one of the limitations), the code for the
\f[I]OFF\f[R] condition will be used to get the required results.
This comes with a small cost as the \f[V]ARGV\f[R] code must first be
run and fail (although this should be relatively fast, it does have an
impact).
.IP \[bu] 2
This is \f[I]not\f[R] autodetected as the point is not to test if
\f[V]ARGV\f[R] is available (it is assumed to be), but if it should be
used for performance reasons.
There is no real way to test this it will be system and data specific.
.IP \[bu] 2
Note that since failure is highly input dependent this is a
\f[I]VARIABLE\f[R] value and can be changed between commands.
This allows maximum performance in all cases if used appropriately.
.SS INFORMATIONAL
.PP
Variables that convey library information.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_VERSION_MAJOR\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Integer >= 1.
.IP \[bu] 2
Incremented when there are significant changes, or any changes break
compatibility with previous versions.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_VERSION_MINOR\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Integer >= 0.
.IP \[bu] 2
Incremented for significant changes that do not break compatibility with
previous versions.
.IP \[bu] 2
Reset to 0 when \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_VERSION_MAJOR\f[R] changes.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_VERSION_PATCH\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Integer >= 0.
.IP \[bu] 2
Incremented for minor revisions or bugfixes.
.IP \[bu] 2
Reset to 0 when \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_VERSION_MINOR\f[R] changes.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_VERSION_RELEASE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
A string indicating a pre-release version, always null for full-release
versions.
.IP \[bu] 2
Possible values include `alpha', `beta', `rc', etc, (a numerical suffix
may also be appended).
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_VERSION_FULL\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Full version combining \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_VERSION_MAJOR\f[R],
\f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_VERSION_MINOR\f[R], and
\f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_VERSION_PATCH\f[R] as a single integer.
.IP \[bu] 2
Can be used in numerical comparisons
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[V]MNNNPPP\f[R] where, \f[V]M\f[R] is the \f[V]MAJOR\f[R]
version, \f[V]NNN\f[R] is the \f[V]MINOR\f[R] version (3 digit, zero
padded), and \f[V]PPP\f[R] is the \f[V]PATCH\f[R] version (3 digit, zero
padded).
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_VERSION\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Full version combining \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_VERSION_MAJOR\f[R],
\f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_VERSION_MINOR\f[R],
\f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_VERSION_PATCH\f[R], and
\f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_VERSION_RELEASE\f[R] as a formatted string.
.IP \[bu] 2
Format:
\f[V]BetterScripts \[aq]libstring\[aq] vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH[-RELEASE]\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Derived tools MUST include unique identifying information in this value
that differentiates them from the BetterScripts versions.
(This information should precede the version number.)
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_LAST_ERROR\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Stores the error message of the most recent error.
.IP \[bu] 2
ONLY valid immediately following a command for which the exit status is
not \f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]<zero>\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
Available even when error output is suppressed.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_SOURCED\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Set (and non-null) once the library has been sourced.
.IP \[bu] 2
Dependant scripts can query if this variable is set to determine if this
file has been sourced.
.SS USER PREFERENCE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_QUIET_ERRORS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Suite: \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_QUIET_ERRORS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]VARIABLE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[I]OFF\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
[Enable]/Disable library error message output.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: error messages will be written to \f[V]STDERR\f[R] as:
\f[V][libstring::<COMMAND>]: ERROR: <MESSAGE>\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: library error messages will be suppressed.
.IP \[bu] 2
The most recent error message is always available in
\f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_LAST_ERROR\f[R] even when error output is suppressed.
.IP \[bu] 2
Both the library version of this option and the suite version can be
modified between command invocations and will affect the next command.
.IP \[bu] 2
Does NOT affect errors from non-library commands, which \f[I]may\f[R]
still produce output.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_FATAL_ERRORS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Suite: \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_FATAL_ERRORS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]VARIABLE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[I]OFF\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Enable/[Disable] causing library errors to terminate the current
(sub-)shell.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: errors stop any further processing, and cause a non-zero
exit status, but do not cause an exception.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: any library error will cause an \[lq]unset variable\[rq]
shell exception using the
\f[V]${parameter:?[word]}\f[R] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/utilities/V3_chap02.html#tag_18_06_02)
parameter expansion, where \f[V]word\f[R] is set to an error message
that \f[I]should\f[R] be displayed by the shell (this message is NOT
suppressed by \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_QUIET_ERRORS\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
Both the library version of this option and the suite version can be
modified between command invocations and will affect the next command.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_INDEX_ONE_BASED\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[I]OFF\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Enable/[Disable] one-based indexing.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: use \f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]<zero>\f[R]) based string indexes
(i.e.
in the range \f[V][0, size)\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: use \f[V]1\f[R] (\f[V]<one>\f[R]) based string indexes
(i.e.
in the range \f[V][1, size]\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
Only affects commands that use indexes, i.e.
\f[V]string_index\f[R], \f[V]string_substr\f[R], and
\f[V]string_truncate\f[R]
.SS COMMANDS
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]string_length\f[R]
.PP
Get the length of text.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_length [<OUTPUT>] <STRING>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]OUTPUT\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the output.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
Any current contents will be lost.
If not specified, or specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R])
output is written to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]STRING\f[R] [in]
Text to be measured.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_length Length \[dq]a string\[dq]
Length=$(string_length \[dq]a string\[dq])
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Requires the current locale is set appropriately for \f[V]STRING\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
If the current locale is the \f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale the result is always
a count of \f[B]bytes\f[R] - setting the appropriate locale causes the
result to be in \f[B]characters\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Although the shell provides \f[V]${#PARAMETER}\f[R] to determine the
length of a value stored in a parameter, outside the \f[I]POSIX\f[R]
locale this often does not work as expected, instead returning the
number of \f[B]bytes\f[R] rather than the number of \f[B]characters\f[R]
- this command avoids this issue.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]string_quote\f[R]
.PP
Quote text such that it can safely passed to, for example,
\f[V]eval\f[R].
.PP
Similar to \f[V]%Q\f[R] format specifier available for some
implementations of \f[V]printf\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_quote [<OUTPUT>] <STRING>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]OUTPUT\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the output.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
Any current contents will be lost.
If not specified, or specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R])
output is written to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]STRING\f[R] [in]
Text to be quoted.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_quote Quoted \[dq]a \[aq]string\[aq]\[dq]
Quoted=$(string_quote \[dq]a \[aq]string\[aq]\[dq])
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Correct quoting is both essential in shell scripting, and also tricky to
do correctly (it is easy to miss some edge case).
In most cases it is enough to quote parameters as the shell expands them
(i.e.\ \[lq]${parameter}\[rq]), but when using parameters in a situation
where a parameter will be evaluated multiple times it becomes necessary
to quote more carefully.
Most obviously this may occur when using \f[V]eval\f[R], or when writing
values via a pipe.
In cases where a value needs quoted, this command not ensures safe
quoting.
.IP \[bu] 2
The command is optimized for the common case, which should as fast as
(or even faster) than \f[V]printf \[aq]%Q\[aq]\f[R] (where available).
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]string_join\f[R]
.PP
Join multiple strings into a single string, using a single \f[V]\f[R]
(\f[V]<space>\f[R]) as a separator.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_join [<OUTPUT>] [--] <STRING>...
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]OUTPUT\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the output.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
Any current contents will be lost.
If not specified, or specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R])
output is written to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]STRING\f[R] [in]
Text to be joined.
May be specified multiple times.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_join Joined \[dq]a string\[dq] \[dq] in \[dq] \[dq]bits\[dq]
Joined=$(string_join \[dq]a string\[dq] \[dq] in \[dq] \[dq]bits\[dq])
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
This is equivalent to \f[V]$*\f[R] \f[B]if, and only if\f[R] the first
character of \f[V]IFS\f[R] is \f[V]\f[R] (\f[V]<space>\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]$*\f[R] uses the first character of \f[V]IFS\f[R] to join arguments
- this allows the use of different characters to join a string, but when
using \f[V]$*\f[R] it means care is needed to check or set \f[V]IFS\f[R]
or errors may occur.
Unfortunately, the value of \f[V]IFS\f[R] affects how a great many
things happen in the shell and setting it prior to use of \f[V]$*\f[R]
means care needs taken to reset it after it\[cq]s been used - if an
error or signal occurs before the value is reset later code may behave
unexpectedly.
This can be worked around in a number of ways, but these often have
unobvious edge cases which can trip up the unwary, while alternative
solutions that are robust can have performance implications (e.g.\ using
subshell(s)).
This command joins the arguments using \f[V]\f[R] (\f[V]<space>\f[R]) as
efficiently as possible, while avoiding common pitfalls, and not
altering \f[V]IFS\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
If \f[V]OUTPUT\f[R] is omitted, \f[V]--\f[R] is \f[I]required\f[R].
The same effect can be achieved by specifying \f[V]OUTPUT\f[R] as
\f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]).
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]string_toupper\f[R]
.PP
Convert a string to upper case.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_toupper [<OUTPUT>] <STRING>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]OUTPUT\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the output.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
Any current contents will be lost.
If not specified, or specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R])
output is written to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]STRING\f[R] [in]
Text to be converted to upper case.
Can contain any arbitrary text excluding any embedded \f[V]\[rs]0\f[R]
(\f[V]<NUL>\f[R]) characters.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_toupper Upper \[dq]all lower case\[dq]
Upper=$(string_toupper \[dq]all lower case\[dq])
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Requires the current locale is set appropriately for \f[V]STRING\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
If the locale is set to the \f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale, conversion is
byte-wise and may not handle multi-byte characters correctly.
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Uses the fastest available method for case conversion, preferring
\f[V]tr\f[R] with character classes if supported, otherwise falling back
to \f[V]awk\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Note that some common versions of \f[V]tr\f[R] do \f[B]not\f[R] support
multi-byte characters regardless of locale.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]string_tolower\f[R]
.PP
Convert a string to lower case.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_tolower [<OUTPUT>] <STRING>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]OUTPUT\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the output.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
Any current contents will be lost.
If not specified, or specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R])
output is written to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]STRING\f[R] [in]
Text to be converted to lower case.
Can contain any arbitrary text excluding any embedded \f[V]\[rs]0\f[R]
(\f[V]<NUL>\f[R]) characters.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_tolower Upper \[dq]ALL UPPER CASE\[dq]
Upper=$(string_tolower \[dq]ALL UPPER CASE\[dq])
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Requires the current locale is set appropriately for \f[V]STRING\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
If the locale is set to the \f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale, conversion is
byte-wise and may not handle multi-byte characters correctly.
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Uses the fastest available method for case conversion, preferring
\f[V]tr\f[R] with character classes if supported, otherwise falling back
to \f[V]awk\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Note that some common versions of \f[V]tr\f[R] do \f[B]not\f[R] support
multi-byte characters regardless of locale.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]string_trim\f[R]
.PP
Trim whitespace from a string.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_trim [-l|--left] [-r|--right] [--] [<OUTPUT>] <STRING>

string_trim [-a|--all] [--] [<OUTPUT>] <STRING>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]-l\f[R], \f[V]--left\f[R] [in]
Trim the \f[I]left\f[R] (i.e.\ start) of \f[V]STRING\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]-r\f[R], \f[V]--right\f[R] [in]
Trim the \f[I]right\f[R] (i.e.\ end) of \f[V]STRING\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]-a\f[R], \f[V]--all\f[R] [in]
Trim both ends of \f[V]STRING\f[R].
This is the default.
.TP
\f[V]OUTPUT\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the output.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
Any current contents will be lost.
If not specified, or specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R])
output is written to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]STRING\f[R] [in]
Text to be trimmed.
Can contain any arbitrary text excluding any embedded \f[V]\[rs]0\f[R]
(\f[V]<NUL>\f[R]) characters.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_trim --all Trimmed \[dq]   a string   \[dq]
Trimmed=$(string_trim -l \[dq]   a string   \[dq])
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
The characters removed are those belonging to the \f[V]:space:\f[R]
character class which contains characters appropriate to the current
locale.
.IP \[bu] 2
The use of the \f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale should be safe all cases, but will
not remove multi-byte white space characters (e.g.\ non-breaking space).
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]string_index\f[R]
.PP
Find the location of one string within another.
On success the values \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_MATCH_START\f[R] and
\f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_MATCH_LENGTH\f[R] contain the location of the
sub-string.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_index [-E|--ere|--extended-regexp] [--] [<OUTPUT>] <STRING> <EXPRESSION>

string_index -F|--text|--fixed-strings [--] [<OUTPUT>] <STRING> <EXPRESSION>

string_index -G|--bre|--basic-regexp [--] [<OUTPUT>] <STRING> <EXPRESSION>

string_index -W|--glob|--wildcard [--] [<OUTPUT>] <STRING> <EXPRESSION>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]-E\f[R], \f[V]--ere\f[R], \f[V]--extended-regexp\f[R] [in]
Interpret \f[V]EXPRESSION\f[R] as an \[lq]Extended Regular
Expression\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/basedefs/V1_chap09.html#tag_09_04).
This is the default.
.TP
\f[V]-F\f[R], \f[V]--text\f[R], \f[V]--fixed-strings\f[R] [in]
Interpret \f[V]EXPRESSION\f[R] as a fixed string.
.TP
\f[V]-G\f[R], \f[V]--bre\f[R], \f[V]--basic-regexp\f[R] [in]
Interpret \f[V]EXPRESSION\f[R] as a \[lq]Basic Regular
Expression\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/basedefs/V1_chap09.html#tag_09_03).
.TP
\f[V]-W\f[R], \f[V]--glob\f[R], \f[V]--wildcard\f[R] [in]
Interpret \f[V]EXPRESSION\f[R] as \[lq]Pattern Matching
Notation\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/utilities/V3_chap02.html#tag_18_13).
.TP
\f[V]OUTPUT\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the output.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
On success, any current contents will be lost.
If not specified, or specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R])
output is written to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]STRING\f[R] [in]
Text to be searched.
Can contain any arbitrary text excluding any embedded \f[V]\[rs]0\f[R]
(\f[V]<NUL>\f[R]) characters.
.TP
\f[V]EXPRESSION\f[R] [in]
Text to search for.
Can contain any arbitrary text excluding any embedded \f[V]\[rs]0\f[R]
(\f[V]<NUL>\f[R]) characters.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_index \[aq]Index\[aq] \[dq]the quick brown fox\[dq] \[aq]:\[aq]
Index=$(string_index -E \[dq]the quick brown fox\[dq] \[aq][fs]ox\[aq])
Index=$(string_index -W \[dq]the quick brown fox\[dq] \[aq]?rown\[aq])
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_MATCH_START\f[R] and
\f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_MATCH_LENGTH\f[R] are valid \f[B]only\f[R] immediately
following this command.
.IP \[bu] 2
Requires the current locale is set appropriately for \f[V]STRING\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
If the locale is set to the \f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale, the results are in
\f[B]bytes\f[R], not characters.
.IP \[bu] 2
Not all wildcard patterns, \f[I]BRE\f[R], or \f[I]ERE\f[R] can be used
portably.
.IP \[bu] 2
For \f[I]wildcard patterns\f[R] the locale in effect is that of the
shell as invoked - \f[I]it is \f[BI]not\f[I] possible to change the
locale of a running shell\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
In some cases wildcard pattern matches will be implemented using
fallback code - this is unavoidable as not all implementations provide
the expected behavior for all expressions.
See \[lq]PATTERN MATCHING\[rq],
\f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_SHELL_SUPPORTS_MBC\f[R], and
\f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_SHELL_SUPPORTS_PORTABLE_GLOB\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Single character \f[BI]ERE\f[B] will be interpreted as fixed
strings.\f[R] (This is due to limitations in \f[V]awk\f[R].)
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Exit code will be \f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]<zero>\f[R]) if the string was
located, \f[V]1\f[R] (\f[V]<one>\f[R]) if it was \f[I]not\f[R] located,
and an error code otherwise.
.IP \[bu] 2
Resulting index is one-based if
\f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_INDEX_ONE_BASED\f[R] is set, or zero-based
otherwise.
.IP \[bu] 2
Following this command, \f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_MATCH_START\f[R] and
\f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_MATCH_LENGTH\f[R] can be passed to
\f[V]string_substr\f[R] to extract the located sub-string.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]string_match\f[R]
.PP
Find the location of one string within another.
Equivalent to \f[V]string_index\f[R] with output of the index
suppressed.
.PP
As with \f[V]string_index\f[R] on a successful match
\f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_MATCH_START\f[R] and
\f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_MATCH_LENGTH\f[R] are set.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_match [-E|--ere|--extended-regexp] [--] <STRING> <EXPRESSION>

string_match -F|--text|--fixed-strings [--] <STRING> <EXPRESSION>

string_match -G|--bre|--basic-regexp [--] <STRING> <EXPRESSION>

string_match -W|--glob|--wildcard [--] <STRING> <EXPRESSION>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]-E\f[R], \f[V]--ere\f[R], \f[V]--extended-regexp\f[R] [in]
Interpret \f[V]EXPRESSION\f[R] as an \[lq]Extended Regular
Expression\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/basedefs/V1_chap09.html#tag_09_04).
This is the default.
.TP
\f[V]-F\f[R], \f[V]--text\f[R], \f[V]--fixed-strings\f[R] [in]
Interpret \f[V]EXPRESSION\f[R] as a fixed string.
.TP
\f[V]-G\f[R], \f[V]--bre\f[R], \f[V]--basic-regexp\f[R] [in]
Interpret \f[V]EXPRESSION\f[R] as a \[lq]Basic Regular
Expression\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/basedefs/V1_chap09.html#tag_09_03).
.TP
\f[V]-W\f[R], \f[V]--glob\f[R], \f[V]--wildcard\f[R] [in]
Interpret \f[V]EXPRESSION\f[R] as \[lq]Pattern Matching
Notation\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/utilities/V3_chap02.html#tag_18_13).
.TP
\f[V]STRING\f[R] [in]
Text to be searched.
Can contain any arbitrary text excluding any embedded \f[V]\[rs]0\f[R]
(\f[V]<NUL>\f[R]) characters.
.TP
\f[V]EXPRESSION\f[R] [in]
Text to search for.
\f[I]EXPECTS\f[R]: - an \f[I]ERE\f[R] with \f[V]-E\f[R],
\f[V]--ere\f[R], or\f[V]--extended-regexp\f[R].
- a \f[I]string\f[R] with \f[V]-F\f[R], \f[V]--text\f[R],
\f[V]--fixed-strings\f[R].
- a \f[I]BRE\f[R] with \f[V]-G\f[R], \f[V]--bre\f[R], or
\f[V]--basic-regexp\f[R].
- a \f[I]wildcard pattern\f[R] with \f[V]-W\f[R], \f[V]--glob\f[R], or
\f[V]--wildcard\f[R].
Can contain any arbitrary text excluding any embedded \f[V]\[rs]0\f[R]
(\f[V]<NUL>\f[R]) characters.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
if string_match \[dq]the quick brown fox\[dq] \[aq]:\[aq]; then ...
if string_match -E \[dq]the quick brown fox\[dq] \[aq][fs]ox\[aq]; then ...
if string_match -W \[dq]the quick brown fox\[dq] \[aq]?rown\[aq]; then ...
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
As for \f[V]string_index\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
As for \f[V]string_index\f[R].
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]string_substr\f[R]
.PP
Get a substring from a string, index, and length.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_substr <OUTPUT> <STRING> <OFFSET> [<LENGTH>]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]OUTPUT\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the output.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
Any current contents will be lost.
If specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]) output is written to
\f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]STRING\f[R] [in]
Text to extract a substring from.
Can contain any arbitrary text excluding any embedded \f[V]\[rs]0\f[R]
(\f[V]<NUL>\f[R]) characters.
.TP
\f[V]OFFSET\f[R] [in]
Position at which the substring starts.
.TP
\f[V]LENGTH\f[R] [in]
Length of the substring in characters.
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Requires the current locale is set appropriately for \f[V]STRING\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
If the locale is set to the \f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale, \f[V]OFFSET\f[R] and
\f[V]LENGTH\f[R] are in \f[B]bytes\f[R], \f[I]not\f[R] characters.
.IP \[bu] 2
If \f[V]LENGTH\f[R] is omitted, the sub-string will contain all
characters from \f[V]STRING\f[R], starting at \f[V]OFFSET\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]OFFSET\f[R] is one-based if
\f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_INDEX_ONE_BASED\f[R] is set, or zero-based
otherwise.
.IP \[bu] 2
If \f[V]LENGTH\f[R] is omitted, this command provides a complimentary
command to \f[V]string_truncate\f[R] - both behave similarly but on
different ends of the input string.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]string_truncate\f[R]
.PP
Truncate a given string to a specific length.
.PP
Equivalent to \f[V]string_substr\f[R] with no offset.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_truncate [<OUTPUT>] <STRING> <LENGTH>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]OUTPUT\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the output.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
Any current contents will be lost.
If not specified, or specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R])
output is written to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]STRING\f[R] [in]
Text to be truncated.
Can contain any arbitrary text excluding any embedded \f[V]\[rs]0\f[R]
(\f[V]<NUL>\f[R]) characters.
.TP
\f[V]LENGTH\f[R] [in]
Length of the truncated string.
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Requires the current locale is set appropriately for \f[V]STRING\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
If the locale is set to the \f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale, \f[V]LENGTH\f[R] is
a count of \f[B]bytes\f[R], otherwise it is a count of
\f[B]characters\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
If \f[V]STRING\f[R] is shorter than \f[V]LENGTH\f[R] no changes will be
made.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]string_sub\f[R]
.PP
Replace the \f[I]first\f[R] occurrence of an expression with a given
value.
(Similar to the \f[V]awk\f[R] function \f[V]sub\f[R].)
.PP
See \f[V]string_gsub\f[R] for replacing \f[I]all\f[R] occurrences.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_sub [-E|--ere|--extended-regexp] [--] [<OUTPUT>] <STRING> <EXPRESSION> <REPLACEMENT>

string_sub -F|--text|--fixed-strings [--] [<OUTPUT>] <STRING> <EXPRESSION> <REPLACEMENT>

string_sub -G|--bre|--basic-regexp [--] [<OUTPUT>] <STRING> <EXPRESSION> <REPLACEMENT>

string_sub -W|--glob|--wildcard [--] [<OUTPUT>] <STRING> <EXPRESSION> <REPLACEMENT>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]-E\f[R], \f[V]--ere\f[R], \f[V]--extended-regexp\f[R] [in]
Interpret \f[V]EXPRESSION\f[R] as an \[lq]Extended Regular
Expression\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/basedefs/V1_chap09.html#tag_09_04).
This is the default.
.TP
\f[V]-F\f[R], \f[V]--text\f[R], \f[V]--fixed-strings\f[R] [in]
Interpret \f[V]EXPRESSION\f[R] as a fixed string.
.TP
\f[V]-G\f[R], \f[V]--bre\f[R], \f[V]--basic-regexp\f[R] [in]
Interpret \f[V]EXPRESSION\f[R] as a \[lq]Basic Regular
Expression\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/basedefs/V1_chap09.html#tag_09_03).
.TP
\f[V]-W\f[R], \f[V]--glob\f[R], \f[V]--wildcard\f[R] [in]
Interpret \f[V]EXPRESSION\f[R] as \[lq]Pattern Matching
Notation\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/utilities/V3_chap02.html#tag_18_13).
.TP
\f[V]OUTPUT\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the new string.
Any current contents will be lost.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name or \f[V]-\f[R]
(\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]).
If not specified, or specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R])
output is written to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]STRING\f[R] [in]
Text to search.
Can contain any arbitrary text excluding any embedded \f[V]\[rs]0\f[R]
(\f[V]<NUL>\f[R]) characters.
.TP
\f[V]EXPRESSION\f[R] [in]
Text to search for.
\f[I]EXPECTS\f[R]: - an \f[I]ERE\f[R] with \f[V]-E\f[R],
\f[V]--ere\f[R], or\f[V]--extended-regexp\f[R].
- a \f[I]string\f[R] with \f[V]-F\f[R], \f[V]--text\f[R],
\f[V]--fixed-strings\f[R].
- a \f[I]BRE\f[R] with \f[V]-G\f[R], \f[V]--bre\f[R], or
\f[V]--basic-regexp\f[R].
- a \f[I]wildcard pattern\f[R] with \f[V]-W\f[R], \f[V]--glob\f[R], or
\f[V]--wildcard\f[R].
Can contain any arbitrary text excluding any embedded \f[V]\[rs]0\f[R]
(\f[V]<NUL>\f[R]) characters.
.TP
\f[V]REPLACEMENT\f[R] [in]
Text to be inserted.
Can contain any arbitrary text excluding any embedded \f[V]\[rs]0\f[R]
(\f[V]<NUL>\f[R]) characters.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
NewString=$(string_sub -F \[dq]the quick brown fox\[dq] \[aq]fox\[aq] \[aq]dog\[aq])
NewString=$(string_sub -E \[dq]the quick brown fox\[dq] \[aq][fs]ox\[aq] \[aq]dog\[aq])
NewString=$(string_sub -W \[dq]the quick brown fox\[dq] \[aq]?rown\[aq] \[aq]red\[aq])
string_sub -W -- NewString \[dq]the quick brown fox\[dq] \[aq]?rown\[aq] \[aq]red\[aq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Requires the current locale is set appropriately for \f[V]STRING\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
For \f[I]wildcard patterns\f[R] the locale in effect is that of the
shell as invoked - \f[I]it is \f[BI]not\f[I] possible to change the
locale of a running shell\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Not all wildcard patterns, \f[I]BRE\f[R], or \f[I]ERE\f[R] can be used
portably.
.IP \[bu] 2
In some cases wildcard pattern matches will be implemented using
fallback code - this is unavoidable as not all implementations provide
the expected behavior for all expressions.
See \[lq]PATTERN MATCHING\[rq],
\f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_SHELL_SUPPORTS_MBC\f[R], and
\f[V]BS_LIBSTRING_CONFIG_SHELL_SUPPORTS_PORTABLE_GLOB\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Single character \f[BI]ERE\f[B] will be interpreted as fixed
strings.\f[R] (This is due to limitations in \f[V]awk\f[R].)
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]-F\f[R]/\f[V]--fixed-strings\f[R] mode can safely be used in
the \f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale with multi-byte characters.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]string_gsub\f[R]
.PP
Identical to \f[V]string_sub\f[R] except \f[I]all\f[R] occurrences of
the expression are replaced.
(Similar to the \f[V]awk\f[R] function \f[V]gsub\f[R].)
.PP
See \f[V]string_sub\f[R] for more information.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
string_gsub [-E|--ere|--extended-regexp] [--] [<OUTPUT>] <STRING> <EXPRESSION> <REPLACEMENT>

string_gsub -F|--text|--fixed-strings [--] [<OUTPUT>] <STRING> <EXPRESSION> <REPLACEMENT>

string_gsub -G|--bre|--basic-regexp [--] [<OUTPUT>] <STRING> <EXPRESSION> <REPLACEMENT>

string_gsub -W|--glob|--wildcard [--] [<OUTPUT>] <STRING> <EXPRESSION> <REPLACEMENT>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.PP
As for \f[V]string_sub\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
NewString=$(string_gsub -F \[dq]the quick brown fox\[dq] \[aq]fox\[aq] \[aq]dog\[aq])
NewString=$(string_gsub -E \[dq]the quick brown fox\[dq] \[aq][fs]ox\[aq] \[aq]dog\[aq])
NewString=$(string_gsub -W \[dq]the quick brown fox\[dq] \[aq]?rown\[aq] \[aq]red\[aq])
string_gsub -W -- NewString \[dq]the quick brown fox\[dq] \[aq]?rown\[aq] \[aq]red\[aq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
As for \f[V]string_sub\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
As for \f[V]string_sub\f[R].
.SS STANDARDS
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]POSIX.1-2008\f[R] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition).
.IP \[bu] 2
FreeBSD SYSEXITS(3) (https://www.freebsd.org/cgi/man.cgi?sysexits(3)).
.IP \[bu] 2
Semantic Versioning v2.0.0 (https://semver.org/).
.IP \[bu] 2
Inclusive Naming Initiative (https://inclusivenaming.org/).
.PP
\f[I]For more details see the \f[VI]shtoolkit\f[I] general
documentation.\f[R]
.SS NOTES
.IP \[bu] 2
The names and design of the command interfaces in this library is
heavily based on that of \f[V]awk\f[R].
.SS CAVEATS
.IP \[bu] 2
This library is design to support any locale that the underlying tools
support.
It is not, however, possible to robustly, portably test for that support
- the library does \[lq]best effort\[rq] testing to choose appropriate
tools to provide functionality, but this should not be relied upon.
\f[B]In particular, the library does NOT have any way to determine if a
locale is entirely unsupported.\f[R][1]
.IP \[bu] 2
Some commands have fallback implementations which are needed for certain
argument/environment combinations.
While these provide the functionality required they may have very
different performance characteristics to the normal version.
In some cases better performance will be possible by using alternative
options, for example, pattern matching using \f[I]ERE\f[R] \f[B]may\f[R]
prove to have more consistent performance across platforms then, say,
wildcards (which use fallback implementations in some cases).
.IP \[bu] 2
For \f[B]ALL\f[R] string commands, the locale of any string arguments
MUST match the current locale \f[I]OR\f[R] the current locale MUST be
the \f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale.
Any other combination of locales is likely to lead to errors.[2]
.IP \[bu] 2
There exist locales which may be largely compatible with other locales
(especially those that are Unicode locales) - however this compatibility
is \f[I]NOT\f[R] universal and differences will exist.[3]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]It is \f[BI]not\f[I] possible to alter the locale of the currently
active shell.\f[R] (The locale the shell was invoked with remains in
effect for the duration of the shell process - locale variables affect
\f[B]external\f[R] commands only.)
.IP \[bu] 2
The library attempts to account for differences between implementations
(where known), however, it is not possible to do this for every case.
.PP
\f[I]For more details see the \f[VI]shtoolkit\f[I] general
documentation.\f[R]
.SS SEE ALSO
.PP
shtoolkit(7)
.SH NOTES
.SS [1]
.PP
For example, the default tools available in OpenBSD make it impossible
to support multi-byte characters properly, but also impossible to
determine that this is an issue.
.SS [2]
.PP
While variables/parameters have no explicit locale the value of the
bytes they store will have been written in a specific locale (with the
expectation that these bytes are interpreted according to that locale) -
this is the locale of the data.
Processing this data is only possible in the same locale, or the
\f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale.
.SS [3]
.PP
The easiest example of this is the various English UTF-8 locales.
While they will match, for example, character code-points, sort order,
etc.
they will also differ in monetary symbol, date format, etc.
(which are \f[I]also\f[R] governed by the locale).
In most cases these differences will not be an issue for users of this
library, but there exist potential edge cases where they may be
problematic.
